{
  "teamId": 109,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Arizona Diamondbacks were a team that spent the entire season chasing a playoff berth they would ultimately never reach, finishing a frustrating 85-77 — good enough in many years, but not in a loaded NL West. They opened the year with promise, going 16-10 in April behind clutch contributions from unexpected sources, but the calendar quickly turned against them in May when they stumbled to 11-17, undone by a bullpen that leaked runs at the worst moments and a stretch of devastating home runs surrendered to opponents.\n\nThe summer months told a story of stubborn resilience. Arizona clawed back to respectability in June at 15-13, then played near-.500 ball in July before a strong 16-11 August threatened to make the playoff race compelling. Carson Kelly emerged as an unlikely offensive force during that stretch, clubbing multiple home runs on the same August afternoon to ignite a push. Ketel Marte was blossoming into a star, and the offense — led by 220 home runs on the season — gave the team enough pop to stay relevant deep into the summer.\n\nSeptember was where the dream unraveled. Despite a solid 15-11 record on the month, the Diamondbacks could not gain the ground they needed in the wild card standings. The bullpen continued to be a vulnerability, with Yoshihisa Hirano and Archie Bradley yielding crushing blows at pivotal junctures throughout the year. A walk-off grand slam by Christian Walker on September 28th — hit against his own former team, with Arizona on the wrong end — encapsulated the bitter luck that plagued this club all season long.\n\nThe 2019 Diamondbacks were never truly bad and never truly great. They scored 813 runs and posted a respectable .757 OPS, but a 4.25 ERA left them perpetually vulnerable. In the end, 85 wins felt like both an accomplishment and a disappointment — a team that played hard enough to believe, but not quite hard enough to advance.",
  "chapters": [
